// soot — funideas §8, the smallest true version of it: a fire that leaves a
// stain where it lands, and nobody wrote a "make this floor sooty" system.
//
// Rows arc down, hit something, stop dead where they land, and each landing
// smears a mark into the `$soot` channel. The marks accumulate where embers
// keep falling and fade on the channel's own clock.
//
// ⚠ The thing you would actually LOOK at is the stain, and this file does not
// render it — whoever consumes `$soot` does. The rows themselves have no look
// of their own here.
//
// `row.stuck` is 1 for a landed row and 0 for one still in the air, so the
// amount IS the condition — an airborne row asks for nothing and nothing is
// what it leaves. The mark is as wide as the ember.
//
// The spray's `casts` could not do this. A cast is one aggregate at the centre
// of the cloud, restated every tick; this is many marks, where the rows
// actually were, that outlive the rows that made them.
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/soot.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.gravity =<cells/s²>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.stain   =<per landing>
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
//
// Turning it:
//   `mul 0.05 | add 0.05`  the mark's width in cells: 0.05 to 0.10. It is the
//                          row's RADIUS, so it sets the collision size too.
spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.k.gravity
collide | stick
perish

// A landed ember is a small stain; the seed gives them different widths.
row.seed | mul 0.05 | add 0.05 | write row.size
row.stuck | mul plane.drift.@self.k.stain | deposit $soot
